idea:
Add a case-sensitive, whole-document "surface format" filter to the final selection only; leave h, both classifier passes, dedup, the cap by p2, cycling and the seeded shuffle unchanged. The current features are all lowercase word features on the document head, so they cannot see case damage, punctuation-spacing differences (the target uses WikiText tokenisation such as " , ", " . ", " @-@ ") or character-level corruption later in the document.

1. Build V = the set of case-sensitive whitespace tokens in target_sample, and df_tok = the number of target documents each token occurs in.
2. For every document that already passes h > h_thr and p2 >= 0.5, compute four statistics over the FULL text in one pass:
   (a) cov = fraction of whitespace tokens that are in V;
   (b) up = uppercase letters / all letters;
   (c) punct = fraction of ',' and '.' characters that appear as stand-alone whitespace tokens (count tokens equal to ',' or '.' divided by the total count of those characters);
   (d) odd = fraction of characters that are neither ASCII alphanumerics, whitespace, nor any character seen in target_sample.
3. Calibrate on target_sample, with cov computed leave-one-out (a token counts only if df_tok >= 2):
   - require cov > 0.9 * the 5th percentile of target cov;
   - require up < 1.5 * the 99th percentile of target up;
   - require punct > 0.9 * the 2nd percentile of target punct;
   - require odd <= max(0.005, 2 * the 99th percentile of target odd).
4. Drop documents failing any of the four tests before dedup, so a damaged copy cannot shadow its clean twin. If fewer than 50 documents survive, fall back to the current selection.
